**Java AWS SDK v2 enhanced client**

```java
@DynamoDbBean
public class Order {
    private String pk;       // USER#42
    private String sk;       // ORDER#100
    private String gsi1Pk;   // STATUS#SHIPPED
    private String gsi1Sk;  // 2026-04-22T10:00:00Z

    @DynamoDbPartitionKey public String getPk() { return pk; }
    @DynamoDbSortKey public String getSk() { return sk; }
    @DynamoDbSecondaryPartitionKey(indexNames = "GSI1") public String getGsi1Pk() { return gsi1Pk; }
    @DynamoDbSecondarySortKey(indexNames = "GSI1") public String getGsi1Sk() { return gsi1Sk; }
}

// Get all orders for user 42
QueryConditional q = QueryConditional.sortBeginsWith(
    Key.builder().partitionValue("USER#42").sortValue("ORDER#").build());
table.query(q).items().forEach(this::process);

// Get shipped orders yesterday via GSI1
QueryConditional range = QueryConditional.sortBetween(
    Key.builder().partitionValue("STATUS#SHIPPED").sortValue("2026-04-21").build(),
    Key.builder().partitionValue("STATUS#SHIPPED").sortValue("2026-04-22").build());
table.index("GSI1").query(range).stream().flatMap(p -> p.items().stream()).forEach(this::process);
```

**Conditional updates prevent race conditions**

```java
table.updateItem(UpdateItemEnhancedRequest.builder(Order.class)
    .item(order)
    .conditionExpression(Expression.builder()
        .expression("attribute_exists(pk) AND #s <> :shipped")
        .putExpressionName("#s", "status")
        .putExpressionValue(":shipped", AttributeValue.fromS("SHIPPED"))
        .build())
    .build());
```

**Document shape — one bucket per sensor per hour**

```javascript
{
  _id: ObjectId("..."),
  sensorId: "temp-42",
  bucketStart: ISODate("2026-04-24T10:00:00Z"),
  bucketEnd:   ISODate("2026-04-24T11:00:00Z"),
  count: 3600,
  sum:   72540.2,
  min:   19.8,
  max:   21.4,
  measurements: [
    { t: ISODate("2026-04-24T10:00:01Z"), v: 20.1 },
    { t: ISODate("2026-04-24T10:00:02Z"), v: 20.0 }
    // ... up to ~3600 readings
  ]
}
```

**Upsert on insert — append + increment aggregates atomically**

```javascript
const t = new Date();
const bucketStart = new Date(t.getFullYear(), t.getMonth(), t.getDate(), t.getHours());

await readings.updateOne(
  { sensorId: "temp-42", bucketStart },
  {
    $push:    { measurements: { t, v: 20.1 } },
    $inc:     { count: 1, sum: 20.1 },
    $min:     { min: 20.1 },
    $max:     { max: 20.1 },
    $setOnInsert: { bucketEnd: new Date(bucketStart.getTime() + 3600 * 1000) }
  },
  { upsert: true }
);
```

**Index**

```javascript
db.readings.createIndex({ sensorId: 1, bucketStart: -1 });
```

**Query last 24 hours — reads ~24 documents per sensor instead of 86,400**

```javascript
db.readings.find({
  sensorId: "temp-42",
  bucketStart: { $gte: new Date(Date.now() - 24 * 3600 * 1000) }
}).sort({ bucketStart: -1 });
```

**Create collection with time-series options**

```javascript
db.createCollection("readings", {
  timeseries: {
    timeField:   "t",
    metaField:   "sensorId",
    granularity: "hours"        // seconds | minutes | hours
  },
  expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 30  // 30-day TTL
});

db.readings.insertMany([
  { t: new Date(), sensorId: "temp-42", v: 20.1 },
  { t: new Date(), sensorId: "temp-42", v: 20.2 }
]);
```

**What MongoDB does internally**
- Creates a hidden `system.buckets.readings` collection
- Groups inserts into optimized buckets keyed by `metaField` + time
- Compresses numeric columns and timestamps aggressively
- Exposes a normal-looking collection API with automatic bucket transparency

**Queries use the regular find/aggregate API; MongoDB unpacks buckets under the hood.**

**When to still hand-roll buckets:**
- You need pre-aggregated fields (`count`, `sum`, etc.) directly on the bucket
- Group key isn't purely time (e.g., bucket per customer per shift)
- You want to control bucket boundaries explicitly
- You're on MongoDB < 5.0

**Product document keeps the 10 most recent reviews embedded**

```javascript
// products collection
{
  _id: "prod-42",
  name: "Wireless Headphones",
  price: 199.00,
  recentReviews: [
    { userId: "u9", rating: 5, text: "Great",   t: ISODate("2026-04-24") },
    { userId: "u7", rating: 4, text: "Good",    t: ISODate("2026-04-23") }
    // up to 10 items
  ],
  reviewCount: 1843,
  ratingAvg: 4.6
}

// reviews collection holds the full history
{ _id: "...", productId: "prod-42", userId: "u9", rating: 5, text: "Great", t: ... }
```

**Append-and-trim on new review**

```javascript
const review = { userId: "u9", rating: 5, text: "Great", t: new Date() };

await reviews.insertOne({ productId: "prod-42", ...review });

await products.updateOne(
  { _id: "prod-42" },
  {
    $push: {
      recentReviews: {
        $each:  [review],
        $sort:  { t: -1 },
        $slice: 10
      }
    },
    $inc: { reviewCount: 1 }
  }
);
```

**Read pattern — hot path pulls one document**

```javascript
db.products.findOne({ _id: "prod-42" });  // 10 recent reviews in main doc
```

**Cold path — 'See all reviews' fetches from the reviews collection paginated.**

**Stored aggregates eliminate per-read recomputation**

```javascript
// users collection with computed stats
{
  _id: "u9",
  name: "Asha",
  stats: {
    totalOrders:    42,
    totalSpent:     3180.00,
    lastOrderAt:    ISODate("2026-04-22"),
    lifetimeRanking: 128
  }
}
```

**Update stats atomically on each new order**

```javascript
await users.updateOne(
  { _id: order.userId },
  {
    $inc: {
      "stats.totalOrders": 1,
      "stats.totalSpent":  order.total
    },
    $set: { "stats.lastOrderAt": order.createdAt }
  }
);
```

**Heavier aggregates via background job + `$merge`**

```javascript
// Recompute product rating average once per hour
db.reviews.aggregate([
  { $group: {
      _id: "$productId",
      ratingAvg:   { $avg: "$rating" },
      reviewCount: { $sum: 1 }
  }},
  { $merge: {
      into: "products",
      on:   "_id",
      whenMatched:    "merge",
      whenNotMatched: "discard"
  }}
]);
```

**When fresh-enough is good enough**, `$merge` writes results back into the source collection efficiently.

**Every document carries a version**

```javascript
// v1 (2024)
{ _id: ..., name: "Asha Patel", email: "a@x.com", schemaVersion: 1 }

// v2 (2025) — split name into firstName/lastName
{ _id: ..., firstName: "Asha", lastName: "Patel", email: "a@x.com", schemaVersion: 2 }

// v3 (2026) — emails become a list; preferred flag
{
  _id: ...,
  firstName: "Asha",
  lastName:  "Patel",
  emails: [{ address: "a@x.com", preferred: true, verified: true }],
  schemaVersion: 3
}
```

**New inserts always use the current version**

```javascript
const CURRENT_VERSION = 3;

await users.insertOne({
  firstName: "Asha",
  lastName:  "Patel",
  emails: [{ address: "a@x.com", preferred: true, verified: false }],
  schemaVersion: CURRENT_VERSION
});
```

**Application-level anti-corruption layer**

```javascript
function toCurrent(user) {
  let u = { ...user };
  if (!u.schemaVersion || u.schemaVersion < 2) {
    const [first, ...rest] = (u.name ?? "").split(" ");
    u.firstName = first;
    u.lastName  = rest.join(" ");
    delete u.name;
    u.schemaVersion = 2;
  }
  if (u.schemaVersion < 3) {
    u.emails = [{ address: u.email, preferred: true, verified: false }];
    delete u.email;
    u.schemaVersion = 3;
  }
  return u;
}

const raw = await users.findOne({ _id: id });
const user = toCurrent(raw);
```

**Optional: persist the upgraded document on read**

```javascript
if (raw.schemaVersion !== CURRENT_VERSION) {
  await users.replaceOne({ _id: user._id }, user);  // upgrade-on-read
}
```

This **self-migrates** hot documents naturally under live traffic, without a big-bang job.

**Background migration for cold documents**

```javascript
// Throttled backfill — 1000 docs at a time, pause to avoid CPU spikes
const cursor = users.find({ schemaVersion: { $lt: 3 } }).batchSize(1000);

for await (const raw of cursor) {
  const upgraded = toCurrent(raw);
  await users.replaceOne({ _id: raw._id, schemaVersion: raw.schemaVersion }, upgraded);
  await sleep(20);  // throttle
}
```

**Enforce the current shape with `$jsonSchema`**

```javascript
db.runCommand({
  collMod: "users",
  validator: {
    $jsonSchema: {
      bsonType: "object",
      required: ["firstName", "lastName", "emails", "schemaVersion"],
      properties: {
        schemaVersion: { bsonType: "int", minimum: 2, maximum: 3 },
        firstName:     { bsonType: "string" },
        lastName:      { bsonType: "string" },
        emails: {
          bsonType: "array",
          items: {
            bsonType: "object",
            required: ["address"],
            properties: {
              address:   { bsonType: "string" },
              preferred: { bsonType: "bool" },
              verified:  { bsonType: "bool" }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  validationLevel:  "moderate",   // warn for legacy, enforce for new
  validationAction: "error"
});
```

**`moderate` validation** only enforces new writes and updates on matching documents — perfect for migrations in progress.
